@@ -0,0 +1,158 @@
+# Empirical PC-path target schema
+
+`get_empirical_pc_targets(data_source, cfg)` is the only supported loading boundary for the
+PC-path matcher. `data_source` may be a MAT-file path or an in-memory scalar struct. The loader
+is deliberately fail-closed: it validates the empirical dimensions against `cfg`, constructs the
+canonical stacked target, validates the covariance or precision matrix, and refuses to guess a
+futures-contract measurement rule.
+
+No production empirical target file is currently present. In particular, the exact joint
+two-PC macro local-projection coefficients and their stacked covariance have not been located.
+Do **not** create a production MAT file from the rounded loading matrix in the implementation
+handoff, stale text output, the legacy AD/RR BVAR responses, or any one-factor LP results.
+
+## Required data-source fields
+
+The source must contain these top-level variables:
+
+| Variable | Required shape | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `V_path_model` | `H_path x K_pc` | Exact empirical contract-loading paths after conversion to the repository's model-rate units and normalization. |
+| `B_pi_emp` | `L_pi x K_pc` | Inflation LP coefficients in model inflation units. |
+| `B_y_emp` | `L_y x K_pc` | Output LP coefficients in model output units. |
+| `h_path` | `H_path x 1` | Exactly `(0:H_path-1)'`. |
+| `metadata` | scalar struct | Versioned scaling, units, contract definitions, samples, estimation design, hashes, and covariance provenance described below. |
+| `Sigma_B` or `Sigma_B_inv` | `M x M` | Joint covariance or precision for the canonical target, where `M=(L_pi+L_y)*K_pc`. |
+
+All numeric matching objects must be nonempty, real, floating point, and finite. Covariance and
+precision matrices must be symmetric to numerical tolerance and positive definite. If only
+`Sigma_B` is supplied, the loader computes `Sigma_B_inv` with Cholesky triangular solves; it
+does not use `inv` or `pinv`. If both are supplied, both must be positive definite and mutually
+consistent. The path matrix must have numerical column rank `K_pc`.
+
+For audit and stable loss evaluation, the loader also retains Cholesky factors and reciprocal
+condition numbers as `Sigma_B_chol`, `Sigma_B_rcond`, `Sigma_B_inv_chol`, and
+`Sigma_B_inv_rcond` when the corresponding matrix exists. The profiler evaluates the quadratic
+form through the precision Cholesky factor and reports its reciprocal condition number.
+
+The loader always constructs and returns
+
+```matlab
+B_emp = [B_pi_emp; B_y_emp];
+target_pc = B_emp(:);
+```
+
+MATLAB column-major order therefore stacks every inflation/output row for PC1, followed by the
+same rows for PC2, and so on. Optional supplied `B_emp` or `target_pc` values are validation
+checks only: they must already have the canonical shape and agree with the construction above.
+
+## Configuration contract
+
+`cfg` must be a scalar struct with finite positive integer fields:
+
+```matlab
+cfg.H_path
+cfg.K_pc
+cfg.L_pi
+cfg.L_y
+cfg.T_model
+```
+
+The loader requires `K_pc <= H_path` and
+`T_model >= max([H_path, L_pi, L_y])`. Every empirical matrix must match these dimensions
+exactly.
+
+## Contract measurement matrix
+
+`M_F` maps a `T_model x 1` model point-rate response into the `H_path x 1` vector of observed
+contract quotes. It must therefore be `H_path x T_model`. It may be supplied as either a source
+variable or `cfg.M_F`:
+
+- if exactly one is supplied, the loader uses it;
+- if both are supplied, they must agree numerically;
+- if neither is supplied, the loader errors unless the caller explicitly sets
+  `cfg.allow_identity_M_F = true`.
+
+That opt-in creates `[eye(H_path), zeros(H_path,T_model-H_path)]` and asserts that the empirical
+rows are point rates at horizons `0:H_path-1`. It is an approximation, not a neutral default.
+The known empirical rows mix FF2, a monthly federal-funds futures contract, with ED2--ED8,
+quarterly Eurodollar/SOFR contracts whose timing and averaging differ and whose underlying
+instrument changes in 2022. Production matching therefore requires a documented contract- and
+calendar-consistent `M_F`. The loader always returns `emp.M_F`.
+
+## PCA scaling and rate units
+
+Eigenvectors from PCA on standardized contract changes are unitless directions, not rate paths.
+For raw contract changes `p_t`, contract means `mu`, population standard deviations
+`D = diag(sigma_contract)`, standardized eigenvectors `V_std`, and unnormalized sklearn-style
+scores `f_t`, the reconstruction is
+
+```text
+x_t = D^(-1) (p_t - mu)
+f_t = V_std' x_t
+p_t - mu ~= D V_std f_t
+```
+
+Thus the raw rate loading per score unit is `V_path_raw = D*V_std`, not `V_std`. If factor scores
+were further rescaled, the identical score transformation must also be applied to the loading
+columns and all LP coefficient columns. PCA signs and rotations must likewise be propagated to
+the paths, macro coefficients, and covariance.
+
+Raw U.S. monetary-policy-database contract changes are annual-rate percentage points. The
+repository's legacy empirical-target convention converts annualized inflation and nominal-rate
+responses to quarterly percentage-point model units by dividing by four. A production
+`V_path_model` must record and apply the verified conversion used by the new PC pipeline. Merely
+dividing `D*V_std` by four fixes units; it does not supply the missing contract measurement map.
+The loader intentionally performs neither transformation: it requires the exact, already
+audited `V_path_model`.
+
+## Required provenance and metadata schema
+
+`metadata` is validated rather than treated as an opaque note. Version 1 requires these nonempty
+text fields:
+
+```matlab
+metadata.schema_version        = 'pcpaths-v1';
+metadata.source_provenance     = '...';
+metadata.pca_reconstruction    = '...';
+metadata.score_normalization   = '...';
+metadata.rate_unit_conversion  = '...';
+metadata.contract_measurement  = '...';
+metadata.target_order          = 'pc-major:[inflation;output]';
+metadata.covariance_provenance = '...';
+```
+
+The exact `target_order` token is a machine-checked assertion that `Sigma_B` uses the same
+factor-major MATLAB vectorization as `target_pc`. Descriptive fields may be supplemented with
+nested structs and exact numeric provenance objects. They must collectively make the target
+reproducible and auditable. At minimum, document:
+
+- immutable source paths or identifiers, file hashes, extraction date, variable order, sample,
+  event filters, and any manual overlays;
+- contract means and standard deviations, the standard-deviation convention, exact PCA
+  eigenvectors, score normalization, explained variances, and sign convention;
+- raw contract units, model units, every unit conversion, contract maturities/averaging rules,
+  and construction of `M_F`;
+- event-to-month or event-to-quarter aggregation, treatment of multiple and
+  scheduled/unscheduled announcements, macro data and transformations, LP horizons, controls,
+  lags, and estimation sample;
+- covariance estimator or bootstrap design and the exact column-major stacking order used by
+  `Sigma_B`.
+
+Optional objects such as `V_path_raw`, `V_std`, contract means/standard deviations,
+`factor_scores`, event dates, bootstrap draws, source hashes, and other provenance fields are
+preserved by the loader unchanged.
+
+## Synthetic demonstration versus production data
+
+`model_estim/demo_pcpath_rank7_synthetic.m` creates its own synthetic `B`, covariance, paths,
+and measurement matrix from the actual RANK structural kernel. It demonstrates that the
+rate-only concentration step and macro-only loss jointly recover all seven RANK parameters. It
+is marked `synthetic_only=true` and `production_ready=false`; none of its objects may be saved or
+relabeled as production empirical targets. The older `demo_pcpath_method_synthetic.m` scalar
+example is retained as a fast unit-level check of the concentration algebra.
+
+Setting `PathDesign='empirical-v-n174'` instead holds fixed the empirical raw-contract loading
+matrix for FF2 and ED2-ED8, while generating `B` from the RANK model at a known truth. This is a
+semi-synthetic DGP. Contract timing is known exactly: the rows correspond to quarters H=0 through
+H=7, so `M_F=[I_8,0]`.
